---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import Answer from '../../components/Answer.astro';
import Claim from '../../components/Claim.astro';
import Disclosure from '../../components/Disclosure.astro';
import SeedWarning from '../../components/SeedWarning.astro';
import AttackEstimator from '../../components/AttackEstimator.astro';
---

<Article
  title="Attack-time estimator"
  description="An entirely client-side sensitivity calculator for published COLDCARD device-search scenarios, passphrase constructions and dice input."
  standfirst="Combine source-labelled device models with uniform passphrase or dice assumptions, then change the candidate rate to see how the result moves. Every number here is a sensitivity estimate built on assumptions you choose, not a measurement of what an attacker can do, and the notes under the result say what each one leaves out."
  depth="plain"
  kicker="Your risk"
>
  <Answer>
    <p>
      Block, Coinkite, LLFOURN and otaliptus published device-search scenarios
      that differ by many orders of magnitude, because they assume different
      things about UID, timer, RTC and attacker knowledge.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        contested
        scope="The published input models and LLFOURN's reported 10-to-14-bit revision selectable in the estimator"
        source="Models from Block, Coinkite, LLFOURN and otaliptus, kept separate because their device scope, attacker knowledge and timing assumptions differ"
        href="/how-it-broke/entropy/"
        compact
      />
      This calculator keeps
      them separate and lets you move the inputs, so what it produces is a
      sensitivity range rather than a time-to-recovery for your wallet.
      <Claim
        basis="derived"
        scope="The derived LLFOURN range endpoints, search-space boundaries and half-space and full-space times produced by the estimator"
        source="The 58.3-to-62.3-bit endpoints subtract LLFOURN's 10-to-14-bit revision from the earlier model; independent search terms multiply; fair D6 rolls contribute log2(6) bits; mnemonic uncertainty is capped at 128 or 256 bits; passphrase and combined results are capped at the 512-bit BIP39 seed output; displayed time divides candidate checks by the selected rate"
        href="#calculation-notes"
        compact
      />
      A long
      displayed time is not a reason to delay: the scenarios remain unverified
      for any individual wallet, compute rates change, and neither a passphrase
      nor a mixed-dice term repairs the original mnemonic.
    </p>
  </Answer>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <span class="callout__title">Classify the firmware path first</span>
    These scenarios apply only after the mnemonic has been classified as
    generated through affected firmware. Pre-regression Mk2/Mk3 mnemonics and
    mnemonics generated after the model-specific hotfix are outside this
    calculator. Start with <a href="/risk/">Your risk</a> if that provenance is
    not established.
  </div>

  <SeedWarning compact />

  <AttackEstimator />

  <Disclosure
    id="calculation-notes"
    title="How to read the result"
    note="the conventions, caps and assumptions behind each number"
  >
    <ul class="node-list">
      <li><strong>Half-space is a sensitivity convention.</strong> The estimator uses half the enumerated space, with a minimum of one check. This assumes a uniformly positioned target and no better search ordering; it is only a large-space approximation.</li>
      <li><strong>The rate is not a hardware claim.</strong> A candidate check must reach the available address, public key or xpub target. Raw device-state enumeration and BIP39 PBKDF2 plus wallet derivation may run at very different rates.</li>
      <li><strong>Combined mode is conditional.</strong> Device and passphrase bits add only when both independent factors must be searched together. If other wallet data lets the attacker identify the mnemonic separately, use passphrase-only mode for that case.</li>
      <li><strong>Mixed dice is output-capped.</strong> Fair, independent and secret rolls can add a separate term to a device candidate space, but a 12-word mnemonic carries at most 128 bits of entropy and a 24-word mnemonic at most 256 bits.</li>
      <li><strong>Pure dice replaces the device model.</strong> The result is bounded by the smaller of roll entropy and mnemonic output size. It assumes accurate entry and no disclosure of the roll sequence.</li>
      <li><strong>BIP39 output has a 512-bit ceiling.</strong> Passphrase-only and combined results cannot exceed the number of distinct BIP39 seed outputs. A particular wallet-data target can impose a lower effective collision boundary.</li>
      <li><strong>Multisig and human-chosen passphrases are not modelled.</strong> Multisig depends on threshold, script or descriptor exposure and whether individual co-signer targets can be tested separately; human-chosen passphrases depend on attacker-specific information. Neither reduces to a defensible bit slider, so use <a href="/risk/mitigations/#multisig">the multisig rung</a> and <a href="/risk/mitigations/#passphrase">the passphrase rung</a> instead.</li>
    </ul>
  </Disclosure>

  <p>
    Use the model to understand sensitivity, then follow
    <a href="/risk/moving-funds/">moving funds from an affected seed</a> for
    decisions about funds.
  </p>
</Article>
